> While this certainly works, it could potentially create a space problem.  The
> clone fileset works (as I'm sure you know, Peter Mayne) by writing out a new
> file only if it's changed.  There aren't that many files in an Oracle
> database, and they tend to be huge.  So you've got the "clone" main DB file
> at, say, 1 gigabyte, and then you change it, and POOF!  you've got the
> read/write copy also at 1 gigabyte.  Total space used:  2 gigabytes until you
> rmfset the clone fileset.

No, that's not how it works. Cloning only writes blocks that have
changed, not files. If you change 1 block in the 1GB file, then the
clone only writes out 1 block. Total space used: 1GB + 1 block until you
rmfset the clone fileset.

> Don't forget to unclone after you umount /mnt too.

You mean rmfset. Already mentioned.
