In article <32DD0425.167E@bath.ac.uk>, "H.C.Lai" <H.C.Lai@bath.ac.uk> writes:
|>Does anyone know how to run programs with bss bigger than 1GB ?
|>The following is what size reports on the program.
|>
|>
|>text    data    bss     dec     hex
|>1204224 122880  1940904656      1942231760      73c41ad0
|>
|>The OS is OSF1 V2.0 240.
|>
|>I got 'Memory fault(coredump)' when I tried to run it. Reducing the
|>bss to below 1GB cures the fault.
|>
|>Thanks a lot
|>
|>
|>H.C.Lai
|>

Try setting the following (/etc/sysconfigtab file):

vm:
        vm-maxvas = 0xf0000000

you may also need to modify one or more of these:

proc:
        max-per-proc-data-size = 0xf0000000
        per-proc-data-size = 0xf0000000
        max-per-proc-address-space = 0xf0000000
        per-proc-address-space = 0xf0000000

#param                  = Original Val  == description                                                  [DU default]
#                       = New Val       date    modifier
#
#vm-maxvas              = 1073741824    == maximum virtual address space                                [1GB]
#                       = 0xf0000000    961208 rmhayman raised from 1GB to 4GB for Oracle's shared memory
#[max-]per-proc-data-size = 1073741824    == maximum data size a process can use                          [1GB]
#                       = 0xf0000000    961125 rmhayman raise from 1GB to 4GB for Oracle SGA
#					[hard] and soft rlimits
#[max-]per-proc-address-space = 1073741824 == maximum amount of address space a process can use           [1GB]
#                       = 0xf0000000    961125 rmhayman raise from 1GB to 4GB for Oracle SGA
#					[hard] and soft rlimits



