In article <32DAA4C7.167E@csm01.eng.lsu.edu>, Rainer Echle <ceechl@csm01.eng.lsu.edu> writes:

>The machine is a 433 MHz Alpha with 512 MB RAM and a 4.5 GB HD. We
>realized that we are running out of swap space which is only 130 MB
>(setup from the vendor) and would like to allocate part of the existing
>user partition for additional swap space. The question is whether such a
>thing is possible without destroying the existing file system.

Nope.  To change the size of a file system you'll need to backup
the data, resize the partition, recreate the file system and then
restore it.  The classic exception is a multi-volume AdvFS domain
where you could remove a member of the domain and use it for
something else.

If you can get another disk, the simplest thing to do is add the
disk and use a partition of that for the additional page/swap
space.  If you have to resize a partition, you'll probably be
better off growing the page/swap partition and shrinking what
comes after it.  Two page/swap spaces on the same disk will
make the page/swap performance even worse if happen to start
paging.

>Rainer Echle

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




