Peter Meckel wrote:
> 
> I have a problem with mounting ISO9660 CD's on our Digital Unix 4.0
> Server.
> I tried "mount -t cdfs /dev/rz3a <directory>" with an ISO9660 CD in
> the CD-ROM (SCSI-ID 3) and the result was:
> I/O Error.
> 
> The CD-ROM worked perfectly during installation of Digital Unix.
> Any suggestions ?

  ISO9660 CDROM's require optional kernel support.  If the kernel your
  system is running does not have support for ISO9660, you won't be
  able to mount the CDROM.

  To build a kernel that has support:

     1.  As root, run doconfig.  When it asks for a kernel name,
	 give a new name.

     2.  You will then be given a list of options to choose.  Pick
	 ISO9660 support and whatever else you need.

     3.  After the kernel build completes, copy the resulting vmunix
	 to root and reboot.

     4.  You should now be able to mount the CDROM (remember to use
	 the special flags on the mount command which were mentioned
	 in a previous posting).

  Instead of building a new kernel, you may wish to rebuild your
  existing kernel.  Edit the file /sys/conf/YOURKERNELNAME.  Add
  or uncomment the line "options CDFS".  Then rebuild with
  doconfig -c YOURKERNELNAME.  After completion, copy the vmunix
  to root and reboot.

