organization: IKP - Linkoping University
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1


I have at present two DEC3000 (one 700 and one 900) that use serial
terminals as console terminals and soon we will have two AlphaServer
4000 in the same computerroom that I prefer to connect to serial
terminals rather than using the graphics console. The console ports of
these are/will be connected to a terminal server. This setup has the
obvious advantage of being remote-administrable over the network and
it also keeps the number of terminals in the computer room at a
minimum - one serial terminal and one X-terminal.

I have two questions about this setup and philosophy when this kind of
DEC equipment is concerned (all systems are running Digital Unix):

1. Can the AS4000 use a serial console? My reseller keeps telling me
   how good it is to have an X-capable console, but I'm not convinced
   - to say the least.

2. All the Suns that I administer responds to some kind of break
   sequence (break on a serial terminal and Stop-A on the regular
   keyboard) upon the execution of the operating system will be
   suspended and the boot monitor is entered. There is a similar
   functionality on the only two VAXen I administer (VAX6000) where
   Ctrl+P will do the same thing. This feature is very useful if you
   have remote access to the serial port and the system for some
   reason cannot be halted. The question is now if the DEC3000 and/or
   4000 has this kind of feature and if this is the case - how can I
   enable it. On the DEC3000 I can sometimes get the loading of the
   kernel to stop by hammering Ctrl-C but this isn't quite enough.

Thanks

-Erik







