x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0Gold (Win95; U)


I agree.  But the user stated large systems.  I assume he meant many
disks and possibly SMP.  Being the Oracle DBA is surely aware of the
optimal setup from an Oracle DB perspective.  Use as many spindles as
possible.  If this truly is the case, ADVFS will be advantageous for a
number of reasons.  The most obvious being online maintenance of the
large file systems and the quicker restart from shutdowns and crashes. 
You are correct about the root and usr partitions though.  There would
be no noticable gain in using ADVFS over UFS and in fact would probably
be a loss due to overhead on the system maintaing ADVFS info.  Another
point to consider would be the OS version in .  With anything under V4.0
all I/O is directed through CPU 0 but I believe V4.0 now spreads that
out.

Just keep in mind that with large file systems composed of many disks
you will be better off with ADVFS as DEC will probably stop supporting
LSM sometime soon as they did with LVM.



