Nik Pallatroni (nik@codman.com) wrote:
[snip]
: You are correct about the root and usr partitions though.  There would
: be no noticable gain in using ADVFS over UFS and in fact would probably
: be a loss due to overhead on the system maintaing ADVFS info.  Another
: point to consider would be the OS version in .  With anything under V4.0
: all I/O is directed through CPU 0 but I believe V4.0 now spreads that
: out.

Nope, AdVFS should be used for all partitions.  Journeling prevents the
accidental loss of /etc/passwd for example, if kernel panics (too often)
during: adduser, then fsck -F ufs / would dutifully move the broke inode
for /etc/passwd to /lost+found where it will be of no use to root who
can't log in now.  

Forget the streched example of loosing /etc/passwd, the point is that 
a journeled file system is more reliable during crashes and is faster
to re-boot.  Yes a 500Mb / and 2Gb /usr+/var does take more than zero
time to fsck a dirty UFS fs...

Good luck.

Curt Smith
