In article <o9pvzi6cvf.fsf@millner.bevc.blacksburg.va.us>,
Robert Millner <millner@millner.bevc.blacksburg.va.us> wrote:
> Personally, I'd be itchy about using advfs for / and /usr.

We've been using advfs for all filesystems on many of our DU hosts for 
1.5 years without any major problems.  Keeping current with the AdvFS patches
has probably been the reason we haven't had any major problems yet.

>  I know it is
> possible to do it cleanly (/ must be a single, simple volume, /usr
> should be the same) but I like to keep booting into single user to fix
> problems as easy as possible.

There are no extra steps required to boot into single user with AdvFS.  With
LSM, there is one extra step required but it's pretty simple.

>  Since you have the advfs licences,
> there is probably no need to use LSM at all unless you need the disk
> striping.

Or unless you want to do software mirroring.  I do root, swap1, and /usr
mirroring on our backup server, and we'll probably start doing the same on
other hosts soon.

> This scheme breaks down badly, as I just recently found out, when there
> is corruption on a volume.  There may be an elegant way of fixing the
> damaged FS but you are better off just repairing or replacing the disk
> and restore from backups.

Indeed.  IMHO, the two biggest problems with AdvFS are 

1) lack of well-documented, robust tools for fixing "minor" filesystem
problems.

