Fred Zwarts <ZWARTS@KVIASV.KVI.nl> wrote in article
<5b2kd0$qpn@info.service.rug.nl>...
> Yesterday it still worked, but today, when I try to login as root,
> I get the message "Account is disabled -- see Account Administrator".
From your news posting I take it you're using C2 security. If so, to
re-enable the root account you need to edit the TCB database.

In DU 4.0 and above this database in a binary form. In 3.2 and below it is
in a text form.

You should be able to log in on the console, no matter what (text console
not GUI), you will get a message something like this:
Account disabled, but console login is allowed
If you still can't get in, boot to single user mode and mount the required
file systems.

( if in single user mode -
  mount / read write:
  mount -u /
  fsck the /var and /usr partitions if necessary then mount them:
  fsck /usr 
  mount /usr
  ...

  set your TERM env var to vt100, so you can use vi:
  TERM=vt100
  export TERM)

Now edit the tcb database -
4.0 or above type:
/usr/tcb/bin/edauth root 
You'll get an edit prompt (:), type vi to edit it in vi

3.2 or below edit the text version of the tcb database:
vi /tcb/files/auth/r/root

then you need to remove what ever is locking the account. This is likely to
be either an entry like :u_lock: this should be changed to :u_lock@:
or, more likely, the number of unsuccessful logins has exceeded the
maximum:
:u_numunsuclog#8:
just change this value to 0.

wq out of vi and you should be able to su/login as normal


> What could have caused it?
Someone trying to login in as root. You should regularly check to see if
this is being
attempted by reviewing the file /usr/adm/sialog
This doesn't exist by default, create it using:
> /usr/adm/sialog
chown root:system /usr/adm/sialog
chmod 640 /usr/adm/sialog

Also a good idea is to alter /etc/syslog.conf to spool warnings to you when
you 
are logged in (auth.error)





