You will probably have to contact HP and have them give you the disk
geometry of a c3725s HDD.  In the /etc/disktab file, there are (or
should be) instructions for how to add an entry for any type of disk
drive.  For instance:

  # OSF/1 Release 1.0
  #
  # Disk geometry and partition layout tables.
  # Key:
  #       ty      type of disk
  #       dt      drive type (SCSI, MSCP etc.)
  #       ns      #sectors/track
  #       nt      #tracks/cylinder
  #       nc      #cylinders/disk
  #       o[a-h]  partition offset (starting sector of partition)
  #       p[a-h]  partition sizes in sectors
  #       b[a-h]  partition block sizes in bytes
  #       f[a-h]  partition fragment sizes in bytes
  #

These are the parameters you'll need to find from HP.  From there you'll
need to add an entry similar to the following example:

  rz28m|RZ28M|DEC RZ28M Winchester:\
        :ty=winchester:dt=SCSI:ns#99:nt#16:nc#2595:\
        :oa#0:pa#131072:ba#8192:fa#1024:\
        :ob#131072:pb#401408:bb#8192:fb#1024:\
        :oc#0:pc#4110480:bc#8192:fc#1024:\
        :od#532480:pd#1191936:bd#8192:fd#1024:\
        :oe#1724416:pe#1191936:be#8192:fe#1024:\
        :of#2916352:pf#1194128:bf#8192:ff#1024:\
        :og#532480:pg#1787904:bg#8192:fg#1024:\
        :oh#2320384:ph#1790096:bh#8192:fh#1024:

Notice how the variable (ty, oa, ob...) all have assigned parameters to
them.  This should allow you (theoretically) to use any type of drive as
long as the parameters are correct and the operating system supports it.
If you still have troubles you might give DEC a call.  They are usually
pretty helpful.

Mike



